  "verbalization": "Cefdinir, a cephalosporin antibiotic, is linked to Slc15a2 through a shared taxonomic hierarchy, with all three drugs—Cefdinir, Cefuroxime, and Cefepime—classified under the broader category of cephalosporins. This hierarchical relationship suggests a common mechanism of action or biological context. The evidence indicates that these cephalosporins bind to Slc15a2, a gene involved in transport processes, implying a potential role in drug resistance or cellular uptake. The lowest common ancestors across the paths highlight a shared pathway, reinforcing the notion that the interaction between cephalosporins and Slc15a2 may be a conserved biological process. The consistent binding of these antibiotics to Slc15a2 suggests a possible mechanism by which they influence cellular function, possibly through modulation of transport proteins. The integration of these paths supports the hypothesis that Cefdinir and Slc15a2 are connected via a shared biological pathway, with the cephalosporins acting as a bridge to the gene.",
